4.2  Inside an SSD: flash, TRIM and wear

TL;DR
  - Flash can't overwrite in place: it writes pages of a few KiB, but only
    erases whole blocks of many pages. The SSD's controller writes new data
    elsewhere and cleans up old blocks in the background.
  - TRIM tells the SSD which blocks the filesystem freed, so it doesn't copy
    dead data around. Run it regularly: fstrim.timer does it weekly.
  - Each cell survives a limited number of erase cycles. Drives are rated in
    terabytes written (TBW); health tools say how much is used.

      systemctl status fstrim.timer
      sudo smartctl -a /dev/nvme0           # look for "Percentage Used"

On your machine
  nvme0n1   Samsung SSD 980 PRO 500GB: NVMe flash, 476.9 GiB · scheduler none ·
            8 hardware queues · volatile write cache · TRIM
  sda       USB DISK 3.0: USB drive, 30.0 GiB · scheduler mq-deadline · no
            volatile write cache

Pitfalls
  - A nearly full SSD slows down and wears faster: the controller has few free
    blocks to work with. Keep some space free.
  - Cheap QLC drives write fast only until their SLC cache fills; a long copy
    can then drop to hard drive speeds.
  - TRIM through LUKS encryption needs discard in /etc/crypttab. It reveals
    which blocks are unused, a small privacy trade-off.
  - Deleted files on an SSD are often unrecoverable soon after TRIM: keep
    backups rather than counting on undelete tools.

Deep dive: 2 questions. Read them with atfq 4.2 --why

Related
  Commands  fstrim, smartctl, nvme, systemctl
  Paths     /sys/block/DEV/queue/discard_max_bytes
